Certainly! Here's a JavaScript program utilizing advanced features like Promises, async/await, and ES6+ features:

```javascript
class APIHandler {
  constructor(apiUrl) {
    this.apiUrl = apiUrl;
  }

  async fetchData(endpoint) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(`${this.apiUrl}/${endpoint}`);
      if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Error: ${response.statusText}`);
      return await response.json();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      return null;
    }
  }
}

const delay = (ms) => new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

async function processData(handler, endpoint) {
  const data = await handler.fetchData(endpoint);
  await delay(2000); // Simulate processing delay
  if (data) {
    console.log(`Processed data: ${JSON.stringify(data)}`);
  } else {
    console.log('Failed to process data');
  }
}

(async () => {
  const apiHandler = new APIHandler('https: 
  await Promise.all([
    processData(apiHandler, 'posts/1'),
    processData(apiHandler, 'users/1')
  ]);
})();
```

This code defines an `APIHandler` class that fetches data from an API endpoint using `fetch` with `async/await` for asynchronous operations. It also includes a `delay` function to simulate a processing delay. Finally, the `processData` function fetches and processes the data, and the whole process is executed in parallel using `Promise.all`.